WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.100
Are two Hulks enough Hulks for the MCU?&nbsp;
We sure hope not. There are plenty more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.566
Hulks to pull from the pages of Marvel Comics.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:12.000
Yeah, he's red instead of green. But&nbsp;
there's a bigger twist: In the comics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.760
the Red Hulk is revealed to be&nbsp;
Thaddeus "Thunderbolt" Ross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.900
Hulk's arguably worst, and definitely&nbsp;
most dedicated, human antagonist.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.820
General Ross is a military man who can usually be&nbsp;
found heading whatever armed forces organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:27.600
is trying to contain the Hulk, so the news&nbsp;
that his obsession led to his own spin on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.580
Hulking out was surprising to say the least.&nbsp;
As Red Hulk, he retained Ross' martial skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:36.840
making him an extra dangerous adversary. His&nbsp;
power set also differed somewhat from his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.760
green counterpart. Bruce Banner's Hulk powers up&nbsp;
when he gets angry, but Red Hulk starts radiating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.080
heat – while still being extremely strong, of&nbsp;
course. He also has energy absorption powers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.980
so he could surely be a powerful&nbsp;
adversary for virtually anyone in the MCU.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.920
Sadly, the actor who portrayed Ross in the MCU,&nbsp;
William Hurt, passed away in 2022. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.360
the character remains somewhat integral to the&nbsp;
Hulk mythos. That's especially true since in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.740
comics Ross is Hulked up by the Leader, who's&nbsp;
set to return in the MCU in "Captain America:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:08.820
New World Order,"and M.O.D.O.K., who will&nbsp;
join the MCU in "Ant-Man and The Wasp:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.360
Quantumania." So Marvel Studios will have to&nbsp;
figure out whether they should give the Red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.500
Hulk powers to another character or risk&nbsp;
navigating the waters of recasting Ross.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.240
Regardless of how they'll go about it, Red Hulk&nbsp;
joining the MCU wouldn't be a massive shock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.522
especially since in the comics, he&nbsp;
has been a member of the Thunderbolts.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.360
In the event that Red Hulk isn't available,&nbsp;
there's always Red She-Hulk, the Hulk identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.520
of Betty Ross, Bruce Banner's love interest and&nbsp;
Thunderbolt Ross' daughter. As Red She-Hulk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.060
she had a complicated relationship with&nbsp;
both men, teaming up with and betraying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:43.560
both in various circumstances. Betty also has&nbsp;
another gamma-powered identity that recently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.220
reappeared in the recent "Immortal&nbsp;
Hulk" series, a bird-like creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:51.840
that dates back to the 1970s. Though Marvel&nbsp;
Studios may want to avoid the name "Harpy."

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:56.460
The MCU version of Betty was played by&nbsp;
Liv Tyler in 2008's "The Incredible Hulk."

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.860
"I will never forgive what you've done to him."

00:01:59.554 --> 00:02:00.687
"He's a fugitive."

00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:02.723
"You made him a fugitive."

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:06.780
Since that film Tyler has stayed busy&nbsp;
on TV shows including "The Leftovers,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:10.380
"Ad Astra" and many others. If the&nbsp;
MCU finds a way to bring her back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:15.420
Tyler would no doubt prove to be a great asset.&nbsp;
If it takes Hulk powers to do that, so be it.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:20.940
Amadeus Cho's Hulk journey is very different&nbsp;
from that of Bruce Banner. He's already an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.320
established super-genius crimefighter by the time&nbsp;
he becomes a Hulk, which he does voluntarily by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:30.480
absorbing some of Hulk's excess radiation. Cho's&nbsp;
initial control over his Hulk is also considerably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.860
better than Banner's, to the point that he dubs&nbsp;
himself the Totally Awesome Hulk. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:39.300
Hulk always wins in the end, and Cho eventually&nbsp;
encounters plenty of trouble with self control.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.560
Cho would be an interesting addition in the&nbsp;
MCU, both as a character in his own right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.420
and as an example of yet another path to becoming&nbsp;
a Hulk. He's also a pretty lighthearted character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:53.220
which would fit pretty well in with the MCU's&nbsp;
recent lean toward Hulk-themed comedy. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.060
probably won't be the very next Hulk to make&nbsp;
his MCU debut, but it wouldn't be all that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.441
surprising if his name was already somewhere on&nbsp;
that brainstorming whiteboard K.E.V.I.N. has.

00:03:01.441 --> 00:03:05.410
"I possess the most advanced&nbsp;
entertainment algorithm in the world."

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.900
In the comics, there are a lot of different&nbsp;
Hulks residing in Bruce Banner's head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.740
and many have come out physically at one&nbsp;
time or another. In the MCU, there have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:18.120
only been two real distinct personalities:&nbsp;
the smashy original and, now, Smart Hulk.

00:03:18.176 --> 00:03:19.316
"You're Hulk again."

00:03:19.816 --> 00:03:20.660
"Smart Hulk."

00:03:21.118 --> 00:03:21.559
"Wow."

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.338
"Heh. Yeah, I didn't come up with it."

00:03:24.338 --> 00:03:28.500
Otherwise, the Hulk in the movies has had more&nbsp;
of a gradual evolution in look and personality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.420
But there's one comics Hulk persona who it&nbsp;
would be great to see on film: Joe Fixit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.260
one of the Hulk's incarnations that's&nbsp;
gray instead of green. Joe Fixit is smart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:42.720
but not in a professor-type way. He's more street&nbsp;
smart. He's a straight-up mob type dude, and has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:47.100
been known to wear neat pinstripe suits and wield&nbsp;
Tommy guns when he's not actively Hulking out.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:52.680
A mobster Hulk just has a lot of story potential.&nbsp;
Just imagine his interactions with She-Hulk, Thor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.000
or the Guardians of the Galaxy. As an added&nbsp;
bonus, the "Immortal Hulk" comics reveal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:01.260
Joe Fixit can also surface in Bruce Banner's&nbsp;
body instead of physically becoming a Hulk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.000
Imagine Mark Ruffalo acting tough while&nbsp;
chewing a toothpick and wearing a fedora.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:10.920
There are lots of Hulk-like and Hulk-adjacent&nbsp;
characters we're leaving out here because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.660
they either weren't created by radiation —&nbsp;
Hulkling is a Kree-Skrull hybrid — or they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:20.220
have gamma powers but aren't technically&nbsp;
Hulks, like Doc Samson and Sasquatch. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:23.760
there's one more key player in Hulk's story&nbsp;
who's yet to make an appearance in the MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.480
He was also a Hulk for a brief while,&nbsp;
though his story goes far beyond that.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.800
In the comics, Rick Jones was the kid Bruce&nbsp;
Banner saved from the Gamma bomb explosion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.340
thus exposing himself to the blast&nbsp;
and becoming the Hulk. Ever since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.480
Rick has been a jack of all trades. Apart&nbsp;
from being a notable supporting character in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:44.100
various Hulk comics, he's acted as a sidekick for&nbsp;
numerous prominent superheroes, including Captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:48.900
America and Captain Marvel. He also gained various&nbsp;
superpowers of his own on more than one occasion.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:53.580
Though he hasn't physically appeared in the&nbsp;
MCU yet, Jones' existence was hinted at in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:57.060
"The Incredible Hulk." If he were to turn&nbsp;
up with Hulk powers after all these years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:01.020
it could very well give Bruce Banner his own&nbsp;
"Captain America: The Winter Soldier" moment.

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:06.180
Introducing a Hulk version of Rick Jones and&nbsp;
giving him a Bucky Barnes-style MCU arc would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.020
not only be a handy way to introduce this giant&nbsp;
missing piece of Hulk's comic book stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:13.020
but it would also be a fun nod&nbsp;
to his comics background. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:16.020
he was Captain America's sidekick,&nbsp;
he actually took on the name Bucky.

